consult a dozen different</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>&nbsp; books in order to produce a footnote (LaTeX =
documentation, editor</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>&nbsp; documentation, operating system documentation, =
various installation</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>&nbsp; manuals of TeX, FAQs for telling which systems =
to prefer and why,</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>&nbsp; and so on and so on).</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>d) working on further usability projects.</FONT>
</P>

<P><FONT SIZE=3D2>I'll not be able to start writing a book before next =
year at the</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>earliest, and Frank can testify that one wants to =
rather have things</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>changed than document the ugly.</FONT>
</P>

<P><FONT SIZE=3D2>While I can't work on a lot of things yet, I can tell =
people not to</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>overlook them so that at some later point of time =
either I myself or</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>somebody else can take up the task without =
unnecessary complications.</FONT>
</P>

<P><FONT SIZE=3D2>That is why I am saying here that the lack of machine =
readability of</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>the user documentation part of .dtx files is a =
problem.</FONT>
</P>

<P><FONT SIZE=3D2>Never mind about the code part: I don't see any =
immediate necessity</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>for having that being autoconverted into other =
formats.&nbsp; Even though</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>things like a LaTeX error hyperlinking to the .dtx =
source or so would</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>be nice at times (but then TeX would need to keep =
track of the source</FONT>

<BR><FONT SIZE=3D2>of tokens).</FONT>
</P>

